WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:12.160
Today we are going to have a look at DeepMind’s&nbsp;
new AI, GATO, which can do almost everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:19.760
at the same time. Playing games, controlling a&nbsp;
robot arm, answering questions, labeling pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.720
you name it. And the key is&nbsp;
that all this is done by one AI.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.320
Now, in the last few years, DeepMind&nbsp;
has built AI programs that solved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.480
a number of extremely difficult problems.&nbsp;
Their chess AI is absolutely incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:43.040
as it plays better than any human. Then,&nbsp;
they proceeded to tackle Go, a game with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.280
even larger space of possible moves with great&nbsp;
success as they beat the reigning world champion.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.800
But, all of these are separate programs. They&nbsp;
share some components, but all of them require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.000
a significant amount of engineering to&nbsp;
tailor to the problem at hand. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.400
their amazing AlphaFold AI contains a lot&nbsp;
of extra components that give it information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.160
about protein folding. Hence, it&nbsp;
cannot be reused for other tasks as is.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:22.080
Their StarCraft 2 AI is also at the very least&nbsp;
on the level of human grandmasters. This is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:29.120
a separate AI. Now, of course, DeepMind did&nbsp;
not spend years of hard work to build an AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.520
that could play just video&nbsp;
games. So, why do all this?

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.160
Well, they use video games as an excellent&nbsp;
testbed for something even greater.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.960
Their goal is to write a general AI that is&nbsp;
the one true algorithm that can do it all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.720
In their mission statement,&nbsp;
they often say, step number one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:57.680
is to solve general intelligence, and step&nbsp;
number two, use it to solve everything else.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.360
And now, hold on to your papers, and I&nbsp;
cannot believe that I am saying this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.800
but here is their newest AI that&nbsp;
takes a solid step in this direction.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:17.680
So, what can it do? Well, for instance, it can&nbsp;
label images. And these are not some easy images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.520
I like how it doesn’t just say&nbsp;
that here are some children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:27.600
and slices of pizza. It says that we have&nbsp;
a group of children who are eating pizza.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.800
Now, the captions are not perfect, we will get&nbsp;
back to exactly how good this AI is in a moment.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:44.080
Okay, so, what else? It can also chat with us.&nbsp;
We can ask it to recommend books, ask it why a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:51.440
particular recommended book is interesting, ask it&nbsp;
about black holes, protein folding, you name it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:58.400
It does really well, but note that it can still&nbsp;
be factually incorrect, even on simpler questions.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.440
And now, with this one, we are&nbsp;
getting there - It can also control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.840
this robot hand. It was shown how to stack&nbsp;
the red block onto the blue one, but now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.200
we ask it to put the blue one on the&nbsp;
green block. This is something that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:22.320
hasn’t been shown before, so, can it do it?&nbsp;
Oh yes. Loving it. Great job, little robot!

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:31.040
So these were three examples. And now, we have&nbsp;
to skip forward a little bit. Why is that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.720
we have to skip because I cannot go&nbsp;
through everything that it can do. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.800
if you have been holding on to your&nbsp;
papers so far, now, squeeze that paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:50.400
because it can perform more than 600 tasks.&nbsp;
600! My goodness. How is that even possible?

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.840
Well, the key here is that this is a neural&nbsp;
network where we can dump in all kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:04.000
data at the same time. Look. We can train it on&nbsp;
novels, images and questions about these images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.920
Atari game videos and controller actions,&nbsp;
and even all this cool robot arm data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.320
And, look! The colors show that all this&nbsp;
data can be used to train their system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.200
this is truly just one AI that&nbsp;
can perform all of these tasks.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:29.040
Okay, and now comes the most important question.&nbsp;
How good is it at these tasks? And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:36.080
where I fell off the chair when I read this&nbsp;
paper. Just look at this. What in the world!&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:45.600
It is at least half as good as a human expert&nbsp;
in about 450 out of about 600 tasks. And, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:53.440
as good as a human expert in about a quarter of&nbsp;
these tasks. That is mind blowing. And note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:59.520
once again, the best part of this work is that&nbsp;
we don’t need 600 different techniques to solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:06.720
these 600 tasks. We just need this one generalist&nbsp;
AI that does it all. What a time to be alive!

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:14.240
Now clearly, it is not perfect. Not even&nbsp;
close. However, this is Two Minute Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.760
this is the land of Fellow Scholars, so we&nbsp;
will also apply the First Law of Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.200
which says that research is a process. Do&nbsp;
not look at where we are, look at where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:31.760
will be two more papers down the line. So how&nbsp;
do we do that? Well, we look at this chart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:39.680
and, oh my. Can this really be true? This chart&nbsp;
says that you have seen nothing yet. What does all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:46.080
this mean? Well, this means that as we increase&nbsp;
the size of the neural network, we still see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.840
consistent growth in its capabilities. This means&nbsp;
that DeepMind is just getting started with this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.080
The next iteration will be way better.&nbsp;
Just one or two more papers down the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:04.160
and these inaccuracies that you have seen&nbsp;
earlier might become a distant memory. By then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:10.800
we might ask “remember when DeepMind’s AI&nbsp;
answered something incorrectly”? Oh yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.640
that was a completely different world back then,&nbsp;
because it was just two papers before this one.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.120
This one really keeps me up at night. What&nbsp;
an incredible achievement. So, does this get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:28.080
your mind going? And what would you use this&nbsp;
AI for? Let me know in the comments below!

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.760
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

